e cases an entire season and not  be lost when you tuned back in that's  because TV characters didn't change in  the plot of each episode generally  followed the exact same formula you  didn't have to devote your undivided  attention to each episode to ensure you  didn't miss a critical detail that might  be important in future episodes or  Seasons there were exceptions such as  the wire and The Sopranos but outside of  cable programming networks like HBO that  had big budgets and more creative  freedom you didn't really see characters  change French scholar Alexi pichard has  argued that television enjoyed a second  Golden Age starting in the 2000s with a  combination of three elements first an  improvement in both visual Aesthetics  and storytelling second an overall  homogeneity between cable and Network  series and third a tremendous popular  success not only did Breaking Bad break  the mold by introducing us to characters  who could change it also helped change  the way TV shows are filmed and consumed  Breaking Bad is Cinematic the second  golden age of Television Drew bigger  Talent not just movie stars but  directors writers editors and  cinematographers too for example John  told the cinematographer on the pilot  was the first person to win consecutive  Oscars in 1994 and 1995 for the movie's  Legend of the Fall and Braveheart he had  never done TV before he did this series  unlike most TV shows Breaking Bad was  not primarily shot on a sound stage in  Hollywood one of the most recognizable  features of the show is the stunning  backdrop of the New Mexico desert the  immersion into this landscape lends a  realism were more accustomed to seeing  on the big screen  who the hell are you  know  you all know exactly who I am  the higher production values were made  possible in part by the advances in  broadcasting and Technology the switch  from analog to digital in June 2009  required full power television stations  Nationwide to broadcast exclusively in a  digital format as a result  HDTVs became more popular the digital  Revolution coupled with higher  definition TVs created more cinematic  possibilities for small screen  production  it's uh it's got that thing where the  blacks are like you know really really  really black  Netflix began to transition away from  mailing out DVDs to the streaming that  we know them for now in 2007. right  before Breaking Bad hit the airwaves the  timing was perfect Netflix didn't have a  lot of original programming yet they  didn't get into scripted programs like  House of Cards until 2013. so their  Library consisted of films and TV shows  from other networks AMC was still  relatively new to original programming  and Breaking Bad had a modest viewership  in its first few seasons on air Netflix  helped to bring the show to viewers and  Breaking Bad remains one of the most  popular shows on the platform even with  competition from immensely popular shows  like stranger things and Squid games  viewers who first discovered Breaking  Bad on Netflix got to watch it in a  whole new way binging and commercial  free viewing approximates an experience  more akin to watching film in addition  to changes in technology much has  changed in the world since 2008 Breaking  Bad is a snapshot of middle class  America in the Yachts following the  aftermath of 9 11 and preceding the  socio-political crises of the modern era  set during the recession and housing  bubble crisis we can see how the  financial and social stressors placed on  Walter White and the other characters in  The Breaking Bad Universe shaped them  while not intentionally a show about  American culture Breaking Bad is a  Zeitgeist show that is representative of  its time watching it for the first time  in 2008 or even a few years later when  it first hit Netflix is a completely  different experience from watching it  with the hindsight of 2020 we are  currently in a global upheaval following  the kova 19 pandemic and the way we  perceive